/**
 * Virginia coverage rows for the Massachusetts Figure 1 heatmap.
 *
 * Shaped exactly like the rows `/analysis/coverage` returns, so the figure that
 * draws California and Massachusetts draws Virginia unchanged and the three are
 * read on one scale. Generated by `server/scripts/va/buildVaCoverageCells.js`
 * from the 32 published Transfer Guides joined against the 16 VCCS course
 * catalogues; regenerate with `--write` and re-export.
 *
 * These are committed rather than served because the number does not come from
 * the corpus the coverage endpoint evaluates. Virginia agreed one associate
 * degree and lets each university pick which options it wants, so a guide
 * already states both halves of the pathway — 60 transferable units in VCCS
 * common numbering, and the 60 upper-division units that follow. Scope is
 * computer-science degrees only — the portal's computing filter also returns
 * information technology, cybersecurity, data science and business analytics,
 * which are different majors with different preparation. Concentrations within
 * a CS degree stay; Radford's four and VCU's three are one major differing
 * after transfer. The whole
 * measurement is therefore: does this college teach the courses this guide
 * names? Routing that through the server's degree evaluator instead put it
 * against catalog-composed requirement trees and a per-institution constraint-
 * proof layer that indexes fixed group positions, which nulled nine of fourteen
 * universities. The join is simpler than that machinery, and this is it.
 *
 * An elective landing counts as covered. "HSS Elective (2 of 5)" and
 * "Unrestricted Elective (1 of 4)" name a slot the degree defines, and the
 * guide has already done the capacity accounting by writing "n of m"; a course
 * filling one is meeting a requirement. Treating them as loss put UVA at 33.2%
 * for describing its elective structure in detail while a university that wrote
 * "Elective" once scored higher for saying less. Across the 23 guides only four
 * rows are outright refusals.
 *
 * Four variants: catalogue vs currently-scheduled supply, each over the 16
 * colleges publishing a computer-science associate degree or all 23. A college
 * without the credential can still teach the courses a guide names; that view
 * is off by default because the pathway does not formally exist there.
 * Concentrations are collapsed to one program per university — Radford
 * publishes four and VCU four of the same degree, and carrying them separately
 * made a university's weight depend on how finely it subdivides its own major.
 *
 * TWO LENSES, both carried, and the exclusion is symmetric: a degree is lower
 * division plus upper division, so removing N units of general education leaves
 * (covered - N) over (total - N). An earlier version dropped the university's
 * free electives from the denominator instead, which shrank the bottom without
 * shrinking the top and pushed 137 of 240 cells above the degree's own ceiling.
 *
 * TWO LENSES, both carried. `pct_named_requirement_courses` is the
 * Massachusetts published measure — general education and elective padding out
 * of BOTH halves, leaving only rows that name a course — and it is what the
 * figure's MA-paper toggle reads. `pct_named_requirement_courses_with_ge`
 * counts general education, which is where a Virginia guide actually puts it.
 * The two land within a point of each other, so the choice barely moves the
 * number; it did once, and that gap turned out to be the broken denominator
 * described below rather than anything about general education.
 *
 * The denominator is each guide's OWN stated size, not a sum of the rows we
 * managed to parse. Summing rows made the denominator a measure of parsing
 * completeness — it ran from 78.5 units at William & Mary to 157 at Norfolk
 * State against stated totals near 120 — so a guide we read less of scored
 * higher, and the column spread was mostly our own gaps.
 *
 * Every unit of the degree sits in exactly one bucket, so they sum to the
 * credential: units the college supplies toward a named requirement, units it
 * supplies that the university applies only as elective credit, units it cannot
 * supply, and units that come after the transfer point. Dropping any of them
 * shrinks the denominator without shrinking the numerator and lets a cell
 * exceed the degree's own transfer ceiling — JMU allows at most 62 of 122 units
 * from a community college, and omitting its 29 university-elective units
 * reported 63.3%. Each row carries `va_ceiling_pct`, the ceiling that guide
 * sets; no cell may exceed its own.
 */

